<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Eliot (missionary)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1604">1604</a>–<a href="/years/1690">1690</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Eliot (1604–1690) was an English Puritan minister in the Massachusetts Bay Colony, known as the &quot;Apostle to the Indians&quot;. He learned the Massachusett language, preached to Native communities from 1646 and organised settlements of Christian Native people known as praying towns, beginning with Natick in 1651. His translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> into Massachusett, printed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> in 1663, was the first complete Bible printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-eliot-missionary.jpg" alt="John Eliot (missionary)" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_John_Eliot.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eliot was born in Widford, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hertfordshire">Hertfordshire</a>, grew up at Nazeing and studied at Jesus College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>. He became assistant to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-hooker">Thomas Hooker</a> at a school in Little Baddow, Essex, and after Hooker fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> he sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> as chaplain on the ship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, arriving on 3 November 1631. He became teaching elder of the First Church in Roxbury, where he served for sixty years. In 1637–1638 he took part in the trials of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-hutchinson">Anne Hutchinson</a> during the Antinomian Controversy, and in 1645 he founded the Roxbury Latin School.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With Thomas Weld, Thomas Mayhew and Richard Mather he is credited with editing the Bay Psalm Book of 1640. To learn Massachusett he relied on Cockenoe, a young man captured in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pequot-war">Pequot War</a> of 1637, who spoke the language and English. His first sermon to the Massachusett under the sachem Cutshamekin at Dorchester Mills failed; his second, in the wigwam of Waban at Nonantum, now Newton, began on 28 October 1646.</p><p>His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> was printed by Samuel Green and Marmaduke Johnson, with the help of Indigenous people including the Nipmuc James Printer (Wowaus). He published The Indian Grammar Begun in 1666, and at one time there were 14 praying towns. His book The Christian Commonwealth, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1659, called for an elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theocracy">theocracy</a>; after the accession of Charles II the General Court forced him in 1661 to retract it, banned the book and ordered every copy destroyed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The praying towns were disrupted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-philips-war">King Philip's War</a> of 1675 and over the following two centuries mostly lost their status as self-governing Indigenous communities. In 1689 Eliot gave 75 acres of land to support a school in Jamaica Plain, on condition that it accept Black and Native American students without prejudice. He died on 21 May 1690, his last words being &quot;welcome joy!&quot; Cotton Mather saw his missionary career as the epitome of New England Puritan ideals, and the seal of Newton shows him preaching to an Indigenous audience.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Eliot (missionary)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Missionaries such as <b>John Eliot</b> founded praying towns, and the Bible was printed in Wampanoag in 1663.</blockquote><a href="/state/wampanoag-confederacy">Wampanoag Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hertfordshire">Hertfordshire</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Roxbury, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.9,-0.2&amp;year=1659" aria-label="Hertfordshire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4545 370 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Eliot (missionary)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4995 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4995 640h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.9,-0.2&amp;year=1659">What was here in 1659? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
